r its points (including points that later become children? Actually, in the standard algorithm, each point contributes to the stability of the cluster it belongs to at each level, but the selection algorithm uses the \"excess of mass\" which is the total stability of the cluster minus the sum of stabilities of its children. That means we compute stability for each cluster as sum over its own points (not including children) of (1/λ_exit - 1/λ_birth). And then when selecting, we compare parent stability vs sum of child stabilities. So we need to compute stability for each cluster using only the points that are directly in that cluster (i.e., not in any child). So we need to know which points are own points vs child points. Given the input has point_exit_lambdas per cluster, presumably these are the exit lambdas for the own points of that cluster (the points that die from that cluster directly). The children will have their own point_exit_lambdas. The cluster's size should then be the number of own points, not total. But the fixture shows size 7 and 3 exit lambdas, which contradicts that. So maybe the size is the total number of points in the cluster (including children) and point_exit_lambdas is a list of exit lambdas for the own points? Then the length of point_exit_lambdas should be size - sum(child sizes). For c0, that would be 1, but it's 3. So not.\n\nMaybe the clusters in the fixture are not hierarchical in terms of point membership? Or the parent mapping indicates which cluster is the immediate parent in the tree, but the points are separate? Could be that the clusters are not nested; each cluster has its own set of points that are disjoint. The parent mapping might be for the tree of clusters (how they merge or split), but the points themselves are not overlapping. For example, in density-based clustering, clusters can be separate but have parent-child relationships in the hierarchy. If points are disjoint, then each point belongs to exactly one leaf cluster. 